  <div class="content-box content-box--tan">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ByteSIM:</strong> {{ providerA.primaryCode }}, advertised as 10% off but tested at roughly 5% in practice. <a href="/bytesim/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><strong>RedteaGO:</strong> {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, {{ redteagoPrimary.discount | lower }}. <a href="/redteago/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Neither code is a huge percentage. ByteSIM's real-world savings landed close to what its own automatic site-wide sale already applies with no code needed, so check the price at checkout before assuming the advertised 10%. RedteaGO's 5% is a public code, not one of ours, but it's the best rate we've verified, and its already-low per-GB pricing means even a modest percentage can still beat a bigger discount on a pricier competitor.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is ByteSIM or RedteaGO the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with ByteSIM if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You need a real local number that can actually receive calls and texts, not just data</li>
        <li>Your destination is one of ByteSIM's supported local-number countries</li>
        <li>You're fine uploading ID and paying more upfront for a number-included bundle</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with RedteaGO if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You just want data at the lowest realistic starting price and don't need a phone number</li>
        <li>Your destination isn't one of ByteSIM's local-number countries</li>
        <li>You want to hotspot a laptop or tablet and are fine with a fixed data bucket instead of unlimited</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>ByteSIM's billing doesn't start until you tap activate, even after ID verification is done.</strong> In our own test, the eSIM didn't come online right away after verification, and ByteSIM's WhatsApp support explained that nothing counts against the plan until activation is actually tapped, then proactively checked back in about two hours later to confirm it had come online. RedteaGO works differently on this front: its FAQ states data top-ups are non-refundable because the plan activates immediately once the purchase is complete, so there's no equivalent grace period once you've bought in.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>"Calls/Texts Available" is the detail to actually check on RedteaGO, not assume.</strong> RedteaGO's FAQ is explicit that most plans are data-only and that voice/text support is limited to labeled plans or its separate Call & Text eSIM package. ByteSIM doesn't have that ambiguity for its number plans: buying the Spain Mega Plan, for example, meant unlimited Spain calls and EU roaming minutes were simply included, not something to hunt for on the product page.
  </p>
  <p>
    For eSIM device compatibility that applies to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide. If a real local number matters more than price, our <a href="/compare/bytesim-vs-alosim/">ByteSIM vs AloSIM comparison</a> covers a real number against a free virtual one instead.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>ByteSIM vs RedteaGO FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, ByteSIM or RedteaGO</h3>
      <p>For plain data, RedteaGO, with starting prices from $0.52 to $2.90 depending on the region. ByteSIM's data-only pricing varies by destination, and its number-included plans (like the Spain bundle we bought at $13.90, $13.20 with the automatic sale) cost more because they bundle in calls and a much larger data allowance, not because ByteSIM's data itself is pricier.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either give you a real phone number</h3>
      <p>ByteSIM does, in select countries including the US, Canada, UK, France, Spain, Australia, Vietnam, South Korea, and Thailand. RedteaGO doesn't by default; its own FAQ says calls and texts are only on plans labeled "Calls/Texts Available," plus a separate Call & Text eSIM package.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Do I need to verify my identity for either provider</h3>
      <p>Only for ByteSIM, and only on its local-number plans, per local telecom regulation; ByteSIM's own site says most of its travel eSIM plans don't require verification. RedteaGO's own materials don't mention an identity-verification requirement for its plans.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either offer unlimited data</h3>
      <p>No. Neither provider was found to offer a flat unlimited-data plan. RedteaGO sells fixed data buckets (2GB, 5GB, and similar), and ByteSIM's plans, including the number-included ones, are sold with specific GB allowances too.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I get a refund if I don't end up using the plan</h3>
      <p>RedteaGO's FAQ says no: data top-ups are non-refundable because the plan activates immediately on purchase. ByteSIM doesn't start billing until you tap activate, even after ID verification, so there's more room before it counts against you, though that's a billing-timing detail rather than a refund policy.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one supports hotspot or tethering</h3>
      <p>Both, in the plans we could confirm it for. RedteaGO's FAQ states hotspot sharing is available in all countries and regions it operates in. ByteSIM's local-number plans include hotspot sharing as part of the bundle.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

